00:05:04,629 --> 00:05:09,270
Now this process is a little different from the traditional supervised learning paradigm

41
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:20,070
where we train models on labeled examples like emails marked as spam or not and traditionally this learning used to be for a specific task.

42
00:05:20,070 --> 00:05:29,749
Generative models on the other hand go through a multi-stage training process that closely mimics how humans learn and specialize.

43
00:05:29,749 --> 00:05:37,110
So here the first stage which is pre-training essentially gives the model broad education about the world.

44
00:05:37,110 --> 00:05:45,270
The model learns from massive amounts of unlabelled data that is we don't have any labels and these could be books,

45
00:05:45,270 --> 00:05:50,629
Wikipedia, public web and so on and this is done without any specific target task.

46
00:05:50,629 --> 00:05:59,510
So in this phase, the model learns broad patterns and reasoning structures just like how people learn foundational knowledge in school.

47
00:05:59,510 --> 00:06:04,950
For instance, a language model might learn grammar,

48
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:13,510
idoms and context and a vision model would learn about spatial coherence and patterns like humans having five fingers and not six.

49
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:27,270
And the result is a powerful foundation model which understands this broad domain of text or images and it understands it deeply enough to generate realistic continuations.

50
00:06:27,270 --> 00:06:30,710
So think of this as some sort of powerful autocomplete.

51
00:06:30,710 --> 00:06:35,510
Now once we are done with this pre-training stage,

52
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:44,230
the next stage is the supervised fine-tuning stage and this is similar to vocational training of humans for specific professions.

53
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:54,310
So here what we do is tweak the model parameters for a particular task or set of tasks using label data. Right?

54
00:06:54,310 --> 00:07:04,390
Now LLM providers like Google or OpenA already do some fine-tuning for very common tasks like say summarization, question answering and so on.

55
00:07:04,390 --> 00:07:12,309
But in some cases, organizations might want to fine-tune pre-trained models on their proprietary data to further enhance the performance.

56
00:07:12,319 --> 00:07:19,990
So they use a variety of methods and these span a large spectrum from updating all of the model parameters

57
00:07:19,990 --> 00:07:29,749
to adjusting just a few using lightweight techniques like LoRa, which is low rank adaptation.

58
00:07:29,749 --> 00:07:37,909
Now I want to mention that in addition to pre-training and fine-tuning that you asked about Priyanka, there's actually an important third stage which is alignment.

59
00:07:37,909 --> 00:07:49,350
So this is about guiding the model's behavior to ensure that the responses are helpful, safe and aligned with human expectations.

60
00:07:49,350 --> 00:07:54,309
So this stage is a bit like giving people performance feedback after they are on the job.

61
00:07:54,309 --> 00:07:59,270
So the model is already generating responses and then we are flagging it as good or not.

62
00:07:59,270 --> 00:08:06,230
And this step uses techniques like reinforcement learning from human feedback that's called RLHF

63
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:16,469
and now there are more recent techniques like PO proximal policy optimization DPO which stands for direct preference optimization.

64
00:08:16,469 --> 00:08:27,430
Well, let's not bother about the various techniques, but the whole idea here is that the models are now trained to prefer responses that are better suited for human preferences.

65
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:40,311
Now, outside the ML community, folks have been using fine-tuning to refer to any form of customization, whether that's supervised fine-tuning or alignment or even prompting it. Right?

66
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:51,110
So that kind of works informally but in technical settings it's better to use fine-tuning to refer to the supervised finetuning step.

67
00:08:51,110 --> 00:08:59,269
It would make sense to move on to the next question which is also important which is the term transformers which is used in GPTs.

68
00:08:59,269 --> 00:09:00,870
What does that do?

69
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:04,071
Right. Right.

70
00:09:04,071 --> 00:09:12,630
Let's see the T in GPT actually stands for the transformer architecture and that is the core model architecture powering today's LLMs.

71
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:25,910
So this was first proposed in 2017 in the famous attention is all you need paper and it's designed around the idea of attention which is essentially a mechanism

72
00:09:25,910 --> 00:09:33,990
that lets the model focus on the most relevant parts of the input when it's trying to either create an embedding or generate predictions.

73
00:09:33,990 --> 00:09:40,550
So typically in language models we encode input sentences as sequences of tokens.

74
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:49,190
Tokens are essentially parts of words and you can think of these tokens as atomic units of the model's language.

75
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:59,110
Now in earlier language models like RNNs, we used to process this input data step by step in a sequential manner.

76
00:09:59,110 --> 00:10:06,790
But in transformers, we handle all tokens in parallel and that makes it highly flexible and scalable.

77
00:10:06,790 --> 00:10:15,030
In fact, this was the innovation that made it feasible for us to build these massive models with billions of parameters.

78
00:10:15,030 --> 00:10:21,269
Now to go a little deeper in the transformer architecture, we have two main components.

79
00:10:21,269 --> 00:10:24,310
One is the encoder and the other is the decoder.

80
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:32,630
Now the encoder takes the input, our sequence of tokens and it processes it through layers of self attention.

81
00:10:32,630 --> 00:10:38,150
That is each token gets to look at all the other tokens and this enables the model

82
00:10:38,150 --> 00:10:47,670
to understand relationships between all parts of the input and as a result we get a set of very rich contextual embeddings.

83
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:51,590
Embeddings are nothing other than just vectors, right?

84
00:10:51,590 --> 00:10:58,870
So we have representations for each of the token in the form of a numeric vector and that encodes the meaning of the sentence.

85
00:10:58,870 --> 00:11:04,389
So essentially we are translating the input text to a vector.

86
00:11:04,389 --> 00:11:15,110
Now the decoder takes these embedding representations or vectors and again uses the attention mechanism to generate output text one token at a time.

87
00:11:15,110 --> 00:11:21,190
Now some of the earlier models that were around like Google's T5 had both encoder and decoder.

88
00:11:21,190 --> 00:11:29,509
Most of the current generative LLMs like GPT only use the decoder and it's optimized for text generation.

89
00:11:29,509 --> 00:11:34,949
And of course we also have models like BERT which only use the encoder

90
00:11:34,949 --> 00:11:46,069
because it's targeted towards understanding the text doesn't have generative responsibilities and you know now we've gone beyond text to other modalities and some of these ideas are being used there as well.

91
00:11:46,079 --> 00:11:48,389
Yeah.

92
00:11:48,389 --> 00:11:56,790
So based on what you just said where we go beyond the text and we use multimodal which is both images and videos

