00:12:04,712 --> 00:12:12,710
I mean I'm glad you brought this up because now we've made a lot of progress on vision models

95
00:12:12,710 --> 00:12:21,110
and most of the genet tools that we use like charge GPT actually support both vision and language and in fact audio too.

96
00:12:21,110 --> 00:12:23,430
So while folks were exploring language focused generative models,

97
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:37,590
there was also this big parallel effort on image generation and this separate evolution came about because of a fundamental difference between language and image modalities.

98
00:12:37,590 --> 00:12:42,870
So if you think about it, language is discrete and sequential.

99
00:12:42,870 --> 00:12:51,509
So words come in a fixed order and that results in a distinct meaning and so predicting the next token made a lot of sense.

100
00:12:51,509 --> 00:13:01,509
Images on the other hand are continuous and spatial and the meaning emerges from patterns across pixels rather than their order.

101
00:13:01,509 --> 00:13:13,350
So transformer architectures which excelled at handling dependencies across a sequence of tokens were a natural fit for a language.

102
00:13:13,350 --> 00:13:18,629
But for image models, we needed architectures that could deal with noise and spatial coherence.

103
00:13:18,629 --> 00:13:26,870
So it turned out that diffusion models which were first studied in physics were a good fit.

104
00:13:26,870 --> 00:13:32,230
So these diffusion models learn to create images using a simple but powerful idea.

105
00:13:32,230 --> 00:13:33,910
And the idea is something like this.

106
00:13:33,910 --> 00:13:36,629
You take real pictures,

107
00:13:36,629 --> 00:13:48,870
gradually add random noise until the picture becomes completely unrecognizable and then we train the model to reverse this process via what we call it dinoising.

108
00:13:48,870 --> 00:13:55,990
So essentially we recover the original image by removing one step at a time, one iteration at a time.

109
00:13:55,990 --> 00:14:06,550
Once we train this model over millions of images, it learns visual patterns that make an image look real like the edges, the lighting, perspective, texture and all that.

110
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:11,910
Once we have a train model, we run this model in reverse.

111
00:14:11,910 --> 00:14:20,949
So we start from random noise and then we do this iterative dnoising one step at a time until we have a coherent image.

112
00:14:20,949 --> 00:14:25,990
Now given some input text instructions or a reference image,

113
00:14:25,990 --> 00:14:35,430
if you want to generate an image, we first encode those inputs as vectors and those vectors steer this image generation.

114
00:14:35,430 --> 00:14:46,790
So you can think of diffusion models as digital artists who are splattering paint all around and then they're sculpting some order out of chaos.

115
00:14:46,790 --> 00:15:00,629
So in essence the model turns that random spray of paint into realistic images one brush stroke at a time and most of the earlier versions of Dali and stable diffusion were this pure diffusion models.

116
00:15:00,629 --> 00:15:03,590
So that's about the diffusion models.

117
00:15:03,590 --> 00:15:18,310
Now while diffusion models were perfecting this image generation we also had big advances in the field of image understanding and that was done through vision transformers or vit.

118
00:15:18,310 --> 00:15:21,030
The second class of models that you asked about.

119
00:15:21,030 --> 00:15:27,269
So the core innovation in vit is that we treat an image exactly like a sentence.

120
00:15:27,269 --> 00:15:38,470
So we break the image into a sequence of small patches or visual tokens and then we feed it into a standard transformer architecture.

121
00:15:38,470 --> 00:15:41,350
The one that we talked about a little while ago.

122
00:15:41,350 --> 00:15:50,949
Now this allows the model to learn not only local but long range dependencies across all the tokens of the image.

123
00:15:50,949 --> 00:15:59,030
This was not possible with the earlier generation of vision models which were like focused only on the local dependencies.

124
00:15:59,030 --> 00:16:04,790
So vits proved that the power of attention mechanism

125
00:16:04,790 --> 00:16:16,470
which really transformed language understanding could be equally transformative for vision and soon it became the most dominant choice for visual understanding.

126
00:16:16,470 --> 00:16:27,430
Now given that we have these tools for generation and understanding the obvious next step was to merge these two and that led to diffusion transformers.

127
00:16:27,430 --> 00:16:41,910
So we had diffusion and we have transformers it's called DITS and these models essentially take the vit architecture and use that to replace the traditional backbone used in denoising in the diffusion model.

128
00:16:41,910 --> 00:16:56,389
So we kind of like merge these two and this fusion is really the bedrock of our modern highly scalable and realistic video generation systems like open AI sura.

129
00:16:56,389 --> 00:17:08,630
So we have now all of these cool models and we have both the vision and language merged in unified models as well because we have transformers and diffusion models combined.

