00:45:21,040 --> 00:45:28,069
because we like literally paying money for every invocation of these models, right?

359
00:45:28,069 --> 00:45:30,790
So, you want to narrow down the list.

360
00:45:30,790 --> 00:45:36,630
Now, within the quantitative metrics, the most important ones are the ones that pertain to response quality.

361
00:45:36,640 --> 00:45:44,390
And these vary again based on the tasks or the capabilities that we talked about.

362
00:45:44,390 --> 00:45:51,190
In case of understanding or retrieval, we might track precision, recall or ranking metrics.

363
00:45:51,190 --> 00:46:01,589
In case of just world knowledge based generation, we might look at fluency, realism of the images or alignment with the instructions.

364
00:46:01,589 --> 00:46:09,030
In case of knowledge intensive generation, we would look at the regret rate, what fraction of questions it's unable to answer.

365
00:46:09,030 --> 00:46:14,550
We might also look at factual accuracy, completeness of the response and so on.

366
00:46:14,560 --> 00:46:28,550
Now for heavily regulated domains like healthcare you know legal advice in addition to these generic typical ML metrics we should also look at metrics around out of scope detection.

367
00:46:28,550 --> 00:46:31,990
So we should not be answering things which are not within the scope of application.

368
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:33,990
Right?

369
00:46:33,990 --> 00:46:36,069
So for that we need to detect it ahead of time.

370
00:46:36,069 --> 00:46:41,510
You know whether we are leaking confidential data or PI data that's an important metric.

371
00:46:41,510 --> 00:46:44,710
Safety of the content compliance with guidelines.

372
00:46:44,720 --> 00:46:59,670
All of these need to be measured quantitatively on data sets and this would ensure that you know the model not only performs well but actually behaves responsibily for our scope and context.

373
00:46:59,680 --> 00:47:13,109
Now in addition to the response quality we also need to assess the average latency the average input context length and output response length for our particular use case.

374
00:47:13,109 --> 00:47:21,190
And that's important because we need to be able to understand the costbenefit tradeoffs of all the models that we are evaluating.

375
00:47:21,190 --> 00:47:35,030
I just want to step back and say that you know this quantitative evaluation with representative unseen test data sets and welldefined metrics.

376
00:47:35,030 --> 00:47:38,471
This is the core pillar of ML modeling. Yeah.

377
00:47:38,471 --> 00:47:46,230
Right now I see a lot of folks who skip this step or do it in an ad hoc fashion and it then comes back

378
00:47:46,230 --> 00:47:57,349
to bite you later because things won't work in production if you haven't done the proper evaluation and in fact I would say even before we build out a solution.

379
00:47:57,349 --> 00:48:02,950
It's always nice to lay down that success criteria in terms of hard metrics

380
00:48:02,950 --> 00:48:12,630
actually derived from the business needs and representative data sets that you know mimic the production setting and we need to implement an automated evaluation mechanism.

