00:49:37,430 --> 00:49:46,470
which is the most important part if that's possible got it yeah so far we were like talking about metrics and the solution from an ML perspective

393
00:49:46,470 --> 00:50:00,230
right but when we really design these systems you need to think about the software architecture ure of LLM applications and the thing that's like working for a lot of people

394
00:50:00,230 --> 00:50:09,589
when it comes to building real large scale applications is this agentic approach and most of the applications we build typically will not

395
00:50:09,589 --> 00:50:23,589
you know be the right fit for one big monolithic model you know because you can't just have one model serving your text and images so it you can't control it as efficiently so when you craft it well.

396
00:50:23,589 --> 00:50:29,829
It is a modular agentic system with multiple components and together they take care of planning,

397
00:50:29,839 --> 00:50:38,390
execution, memory, reasoning, actions and even reflection and learning over time.

398
00:50:38,390 --> 00:50:44,870
So to understand what's going on, imagine a well-run project team where each component maps to a specific role.

399
00:50:44,880 --> 00:50:53,829
So at the top we have the planner or the project head and this is the person who understands the overall requirements

400
00:50:53,829 --> 00:51:04,390
and translates those requirements into a structured plan breaking the work down into smaller actionable tasks that the rest of the team can execute.

401
00:51:04,390 --> 00:51:08,630
Note that the planner doesn't actually do the work.

402
00:51:08,630 --> 00:51:11,670
The planner only defines what needs to be done and in what order.

403
00:51:11,670 --> 00:51:14,150
Now this is where the orchestrator steps in.

404
00:51:14,160 --> 00:51:28,950
So the orchestrator is like the program manager, the one who ensures that the plan actually gets executed by coordinating between the various members of the team or in our case the other components of the system.

405
00:51:28,950 --> 00:51:40,069
So this orchestrator is not just a messenger keeps an eye on the progress and if something goes wrong can go back to the planner and you know re-trigger the replanning.

406
00:51:40,069 --> 00:51:44,230
So together the planner and orchestrator form the control loop of the system.

407
00:51:44,230 --> 00:51:49,430
So the planner designs the road map and the orchestrator ensures that the journey stays on track.

408
00:51:49,430 --> 00:51:52,150
Now supporting these two is memory.

409
00:51:52,150 --> 00:52:06,230
Think of this as you know the project archive assistant who's responsible for maintaining the meeting note all the decisions task updates so that the everybody in the team has the right context.

410
00:52:06,230 --> 00:52:11,430
Now without memory the system might forget the context or repeat unnecessary work or repeat errors.

411
00:52:11,430 --> 00:52:24,150
But with memory the agent can reason pretty coherently across sessions or in case of chat you know across various turns of conversation.

412
00:52:24,150 --> 00:52:33,030
Now at the core of all of this planning and orchestration memory we actually have something even more important which is the LLM itself.

413
00:52:33,030 --> 00:52:38,870
Think of this as the expert strategist or like the Yoda of the team.

414
00:52:38,870 --> 00:52:50,549
This is the systems reasoning and thinking engine and this is the one that interprets the goals, reflects on the past outcomes and it provides intelligent guidance.

415
00:52:50,549 --> 00:52:59,750
So this module doesn't actually execute anything but it's the central intelligence that everyone else depends on the for guidance.

416
00:52:59,750 --> 00:53:02,230
So this works behind the scene.

417
00:53:02,230 --> 00:53:05,750
Now apart from all of these four core components,

418
00:53:05,750 --> 00:53:15,109
we also have a tool layer which typically varies from application to application and these are like the specialist members of the team.

419
00:53:15,119 --> 00:53:27,030
So the tools could be something like the programmers or the graphic designers, business analysts and they do specific jobs which is assigned to them by the orchestrator.

420
00:53:27,030 --> 00:53:33,349
Now in an automated system these tools are APIs or external models or any kind of task specific components.

