01:07:33,359 --> 01:07:46,150
while back is that you know a lot of these techniques have been incorporated into these models and the focus actually has shifted towards automated prompt optimization.

541
01:07:46,150 --> 01:07:54,710
So automated prompt optimization essentially refers to methods that do away with the manual tweaking of prompts.

542
01:07:54,710 --> 01:08:00,069
See in earlier days people used to train models you know with labeled examples.

543
01:08:00,069 --> 01:08:04,870
So we would have like let's take a simple classification model.

544
01:08:04,870 --> 01:08:09,910
We would give a labeled examples and then the model would look at the examples and learn the parameters.

545
01:08:09,910 --> 01:08:18,630
Now what's happening with this foundation models is that they've kind of become like a black box to us and the only thing that we can tweak is the prompt.

546
01:08:18,640 --> 01:08:29,510
So prompt has essentially become our parameter and what folks have figured out is a way to automatically tweak the prompts based on a set of input

547
01:08:29,510 --> 01:08:34,390
and output examples and you know some of you might know about gradient descent right.

548
01:08:34,400 --> 01:08:47,590
So gradient descent is a learning algorithm where we you know take the old parameters we look at the loss function on our label data and based on that we update the parameters to give a new set.

549
01:08:47,600 --> 01:08:56,470
So similar to that we now have techniques which are based on text gradients where we look at the old prompt.

550
01:08:56,470 --> 01:09:09,829
We look at the you know loss or some metric on a given set of labeled examples and then we update this prompt just said all of this is happening in the language space and then we get a new prompt.

551
01:09:09,829 --> 01:09:12,630
Now there are a bunch of tools out there.

552
01:09:12,630 --> 01:09:21,269
I particular want to call out this framework called DSPY and there's a nice software not just like a package but also a community around this.

553
01:09:21,269 --> 01:09:36,149
So DSPY stands for declarative self-improving language programs and here you can declare how you want your system to be and it's not just a one model but it could be a fairly complex agentic system.

554
01:09:36,149 --> 01:09:40,229
I mean it could be even be a simple one but it could also be a complex one.

555
01:09:40,229 --> 01:09:51,590
You can just declare the architecture of your system and then it automatically writes the low-level prompts and also optimizes them once you give it a bunch of label data.

556
01:09:51,590 --> 01:09:57,591
There's actually a lot of research going on in this space but maybe we can get to it later if we have time. Yeah.

