569
01:11:16,390 --> 01:11:23,669
3D depth and even infrared thermal signals and these are particularly useful for robotics and embodied AI systems.

570
01:11:23,669 --> 01:11:26,709
There are also breakthroughs in artificial smell.

571
01:11:26,709 --> 01:11:31,830
So researchers call these electronic noses and these systems use chemical sensors

572
01:11:31,840 --> 01:11:42,390
to detect organic compounds in the air and they can pick up a lot of things that would be completely obvious for us right from detecting gas leaks,

573
01:11:42,400 --> 01:11:48,870
monitoring air quality to analyzing human breath for signs of disease.

574
01:11:48,870 --> 01:11:54,390
So there's a lot that can be done and these are quite powerful.

575
01:11:54,390 --> 01:11:56,390
I want to also mention something interesting.

576
01:11:56,390 --> 01:11:59,590
There is this notion of internet of senses.

577
01:11:59,590 --> 01:12:03,030
You know iOS but a different iOS.

578
01:12:03,030 --> 01:12:14,229
And here the idea is that the AI model can serve as a cognitive hub or an artificial brain

579
01:12:14,229 --> 01:12:25,590
that can connect and interpret all of these sensory inputs visual audio allactory haptic and so on and build a deep contextual understanding of the physical world.

580
01:12:25,590 --> 01:12:37,270
For instance, if you think of what we understand as an apple, it is, you know, the visual input, how it looks, how it smells, the touch, all of that contribute to it.

581
01:12:37,270 --> 01:12:46,790
And what people are trying to do is, you know, capture these signals through various sensors and reconstruct that conceptualization.

582
01:12:46,790 --> 01:12:49,669
Now, that is the understanding part.

583
01:12:49,669 --> 01:12:56,470
Now the next step is to go beyond understanding and recreate an artificial apple

584
01:12:56,470 --> 01:13:06,310
or the sensation of an apple right for somebody else and that's likely to lead to really rich immersive multi-ensory experiences.

585
01:13:06,310 --> 01:13:12,070
Now, one other thing to remember here is that as these AI systems begin

586
01:13:12,070 --> 01:13:22,709
to integrate sensors which will capture signals across the sound and electromagnetic spectrum beyond the visible audible range, right?

587
01:13:22,709 --> 01:13:34,310
It will gain the ability to sense things that we can't like, you know, things that are invisible, inaudible to us, things we might not be able to smell or taste.

588
01:13:34,310 --> 01:13:46,149
And that opens up extraordinary possibilities which is to some extent scary because they will be able to perceive the world at a level much beyond our own capacities.

589
01:13:46,159 --> 01:13:48,550
Pretty scary.

590
01:13:48,550 --> 01:13:56,390
And I think that's something that we all have to watch out for of what the world brings to us with all this innovation.

591
01:13:56,390 --> 01:13:59,590
I want to end the show by asking you a question.

592
01:13:59,590 --> 01:14:05,510
Every day there seems to be a new innovation, a newer way of doing things.

593
01:14:05,510 --> 01:14:09,350
So how to keep up with this fast pace of innovation?

594
01:14:09,360 --> 01:14:19,990
Oh yeah, that's so pertinent and honestly it is difficult for all of us and you know I I do find the pace of innovation very dizzying

595
01:14:19,990 --> 01:14:25,350
because there's just so much happening with new tools, new models, new papers.

596
01:14:25,350 --> 01:14:31,270
It literally feels like you are at the receiving end of a fire hose.

597
01:14:31,270 --> 01:14:33,590
So I have my coping mechanisms and I'm sure everybody does.

598
01:14:33,600 --> 01:14:36,470
So I could definitely share these.

599
01:14:36,470 --> 01:14:49,590
First thing I feel is we need to make peace with the fact that the old education paradigm where we study for a few years you know school and college and then coast through the rest of our career.

600
01:14:49,590 --> 01:14:53,350
So that one is gone.

601
01:14:53,350 --> 01:15:05,430
So we definitely have to be lifelong learners and at the same time we also need to accept that we cannot stay on top of everything and you know

602
01:15:05,430 --> 01:15:14,149
because the and the other thing is it might not be important to do that the halflife of knowledge has become so short

603
01:15:14,149 --> 01:15:20,870
that there actually is no point learning a lot of things that we are not going to use right away because it's anyway going to change.

604
01:15:20,870 --> 01:15:27,990
So what seems to matter a lot more now is figuring out how to learn and adapt.

605
01:15:27,990 --> 01:15:34,870
And if you just look online, there's a lot of noise and very little good signal.

606
01:15:34,870 --> 01:15:44,229
And so it's important for us to focus on number one, what do we really want to acquire in terms of knowledge, right?

607
01:15:44,229 --> 01:15:54,870
So it helps to curate some trustworthy sources could be newsletters could be people authoritative people that you follow or some forums that you participate in.

608
01:15:54,870 --> 01:15:58,149
So curation of sources is important.

609
01:15:58,149 --> 01:16:06,390
The second one is having a mechanism to sample what's going on and this could be you know a periodic activity.

610
01:16:06,390 --> 01:16:17,910
In my case, just a few minutes a day, I just look through TLDDR and some LinkedIn posts and that gives me a sense of what new things are coming up.

611
01:16:17,910 --> 01:16:22,630
And even if I don't have the bandwidth to really deep dive there,

612
01:16:22,630 --> 01:16:31,830
I just kind of like bookmark it for later and maybe once a week I go back to look at some of the interesting things.

613
01:16:31,830 --> 01:16:38,070
Now apart from this I do deep dive into topics that I'm really curious about or I'm going to work on soon.

614
01:16:38,070 --> 01:16:44,870
And one more thing that's important is there's a lot out there that's not being peer reviewed.

615
01:16:44,870 --> 01:16:48,310
So we really have to be critical in how we assess the information that's coming to us.

616
01:16:48,310 --> 01:16:58,709
You know there might be great ideas there might be also flawed ideas and it's important us to for us to have our thinking caps on in terms of tooling.

617
01:16:58,719 --> 01:17:03,189
Priyanka I feel like tools like notion notebook llm they are pretty good.

618
01:17:03,189 --> 01:17:05,270
So those are things that we could use.

619
01:17:05,280 --> 01:17:15,590
I think that's a good thing that you've given us in terms of what we could look at things a bit more critically and curate your knowledge sources.

620
